    <Placemark>
      <name>TAIKO-LAB Aoyama</name>
      <description>Dedicated taiko school offering a 60-minute English-language hands-on Japanese drumming experience.

Why: Very easy to integrate into a Tokyo trip: professional instruction, drums and sticks are provided, beginners are welcome regardless of age, and the session builds toward playing a complete piece before an instructor performs a taiko solo.

Drums / Do / Aoyama
(pin is area-level, not the exact address)
https://www.taikolab.com/world/</description>
      <styleUrl>#theme-drums</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.714,35.67,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Oedo Sukeroku Taiko Dojo</name>
      <description>Private and small-group taiko lessons taught in English or Japanese by professional Oedo Sukeroku Taiko players.

Why: A more traditional and serious alternative to a packaged visitor experience. The dojo explicitly offers 1- and 1.5-hour lessons for travelers and families, including complete beginners, and lets you learn directly from professional performers in the influential Oedo Sukeroku taiko tradition.

Drums / Do / Sotokanda
(pin is area-level, not the exact address)
https://oedosukerokutaiko.com/htm/school_foreigner_english.html</description>
      <styleUrl>#theme-drums</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.769,35.7,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>HIBIKUS Asakusa Basement</name>
      <description>Taiko school and workshop studio beneath the headquarters of historic Japanese drum maker Miyamoto Unosuke Shoten.

Why: Especially appealing if staying in Asakusa: this is a real taiko school rather than a tourist attraction, professional players teach there, and the school accepts students from age three upward. The unusual basement studio occupies a historic space once used to store mikoshi and later as an air-raid shelter.

Drums / Do / Asakusa
(pin is area-level, not the exact address)
https://www.hibikus.com/taiko-school/asakusa</description>
      <styleUrl>#theme-drums</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.8,35.717,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Taikokan Drum Museum</name>
      <description>Small specialist museum created by Miyamoto Unosuke Shoten with hundreds of Japanese and international drums, including instruments visitors can actually play.

Why: A particularly good companion to a taiko lesson for a drummer: the collection contains roughly 800 drums, selected exhibits marked with a musical-note symbol can be played, and the Miyamoto Unosuke shop downstairs sells taiko, bachi and other traditional percussion equipment.

Drums / See / Nishi-Asakusa
(pin is area-level, not the exact address)
https://www.miyamoto-unosuke.co.jp/pages/museum</description>
      <styleUrl>#theme-drums</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.79,35.711,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>ZAKURO SHOW — October 23, 2026</name>
      <description>October 23, 2026 bilingual one-hour live concert featuring taiko alongside koto, shamisen, shakuhachi and other traditional Japanese instruments.

Why: Confirmed for October 23 during the Tokyo trip. It is an intimate performance with top-level traditional musicians only a few feet from the audience, with English explanations of the instruments and music. This is broader than a pure taiko concert but provides a strong live-performance counterpart to a hands-on taiko lesson.

Drums / See / Harajuku
(pin is area-level, not the exact address)
https://www.zakuroshow.com/en</description>
      <styleUrl>#theme-drums</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.703,35.669,0</coordinates></Point>
